# The scripts are edited on Windows and run on Linux in the container, so every tracked file is stored
# AND checked out with LF endings, whatever a clone's core.autocrlf happens to be set to. Attributes
# beat that setting, so nobody has to configure anything.
#
# Marking a file `text` alone is not enough: it normalises to LF in the repository but still checks out
# as CRLF on Windows under autocrlf=true, which is why git kept warning that it would change these files
# back on the next checkout, and why some working copies were CRLF and others LF. `eol=lf` pins both
# ends, so what is on disk is what is committed and one file cannot drift from the next.
#
# text=auto still leaves anything that looks binary alone.
* text=auto eol=lf
